it's cold and dark a bitter unrelenting  snow surrounds you it's 3:00 a.m. and  you've been walking through downtown  Boston for over an hour you went to MIT  and you launched a successful startup  but that's over now  and all you can ask yourself is how did  I end up here you don't know and you  can't sleep see you walk that frigid two  and a half miles in the middle of the  night this is my life in November 2012 I  spent two years in the trenches building  my first real startup finally tasted  success only to watch it drift away I  had learned what it meant to rise and  now I was learning what it means to fall  Abraham Lincoln said that we are greedy  to publish the success of our efforts  but shy as to publishing our failures  and that we were ruined by this  one-sided practice of concealment of  blunders and failures for me that ends  today  this is a talk about falling one 19 1999  my family and I were about to drive back  home from dinner  I was busy playing one of the greatest  games of all time pokemon red that's a  great game  I caught a new Pokemon I was so excited  I ran into my mom but I didn't quite  make it  see I don't remember seeing the curb in  the parking lot that night but I  distinctly recall the taste of cement  when my face with the ground falling is  painful my mom picked me up and saw that  one of my two front teeth had been  pushed down and was now the better part  of two millimeters south of where it  should be not good we rushed to the  emergency room they told us to call the  dentist and he said I can see you in the  morning but by then it'll be too late if  you want to fix this you're going to  push it you're gonna have to push it  back up yourself I was ten  but these were permanent teeth and if  you feel queasy right now imagine how I  felt  I grabbed my mom's hand and squeezed as  hard as I could cry and pushing it one  two millimeters back into place and it  worked  see sometimes falling isn't a choice  but getting back up is investors and  startups often say that one of the  biggest risks and early-stage companies  is the team I couldn't agree more  let's go back to 2012 it was a bright  day in August and I went for a walk with  my co-founder as I often did but this  time something wasn't quite right the  summary had been a roller coaster ups  and downs and I could just feel it as we  were walking something was very wrong we  sat down to talk and I looked at him and  said what I could not believe but knew  to be true look you're gonna fire me  just do it and he did we started this  together and he fired me I went back to  the office at night to get my things and  I looked around and saw the company I'd  helped build and the products I'd helped  create and in that moment I felt like  I'd lost part of myself leaving the  startup was complicated  we both had leverage and we began to  negotiate I'd never done this before and  I made just about every mistake in the  book every time we come close to  reaching an agreement I just be  overtaken by fear and ask for something  new something more what should have  taken weeks turn into months I was  winning the negotiation mind you but at  the same time I was losing what was left  of our friendship I felt terrible but I  couldn't find a way out by Thanksgiving  I was burnout I was done what it started  as a simple negotiation  and become a chronic illness with no  cure but sometimes it's our darkest  moments that provide us with the  greatest clarity and from that low  vantage point it all made sense I had  become so afraid in Boston I hadn't just  lost my startup I had lost myself I had  forgotten about the future I knew then  that I had to stop trying to win so  that's what I did I forgave my friend  and I forgave myself fewer shares would  be enough this changed everything  between us and in that moment we became  old friends again and he even agreed to  help me sell enough shares to take a  year off you know it was one of those  win-win scenarios have always heard  about they actually do exist the process  wasn't easy but by the end of 2012 I was  free you know my greatest fear in  leaving the startup was not the money or  my reputation my greatest fear was  becoming a guy named Pete best now Pete  best was the first the original drummer  for The Beatles but you may not have  heard of him not because he missed out  but because shortly after leaving the  Beatles he didn't play a single show for  20 years Steve Jobs was fired from Apple  only after making the cover of Time  magazine but because he kept growing and  learning he ended up on the cover not  one but five more times I had a choice  to make when I keep growing would I keep  learning in my ear off I moved to Austin  Texas to build a live and work design  studio with my friends it was awesome  and Austin is an incredible place I  chose I chose Austin because it it's the  city where it's whatever you need it to  be and it would be my place to recharge  we ended up using the studio as this  incredible retreat and we invited all  kinds  people down to visit us entrepreneurs  musicians engineers screenwriters and  even a National Spelling Bee champion  King it was awesome  seriously there aren't that many but you  know we together we talked about falling  and what it takes to get back up again  and again I discovered that this is  called grit University of Pennsylvania  professor Angela Duckworth defines grit  as perseverance and passion for  long-term goals as she says that these  goals can take years even decades to  complete and her research shows that  this requires unwavering focus but wait  a minute wait a minute  if being gritty means sticking with your  goals then what about leaving the start  up how do these things match up so when  I got the chance to interview her I  asked about flexibility professor  Duckworth told me that less gritty  people have it backwards there were  little too flexible about their long  term goals and at the same time too  rigid about how they get their day to  day gritty people on the other hand or  the complete and total opposite dead-set  on their long term vision and at the  same time pretty flexible about how it  happens when you know where you want to  end up exactly which path you take is  less important on my path I had fallen  and though my time in Austin was an  absolute gift I knew that I couldn't  rest forever I came to Stanford to learn  about the future of hardware I came to  Stanford to learn about how to start  consumer brands that enchant the world I  came to Stanford to get back up and as  you walk on your path I can almost  guarantee that you will fall and then it  will hurt but remember that it's okay to  be flexible and sometimes you know it's  not just okay it's necessary grit is  having the patience and tenacity to keep  after it until you can get there no  matter which way you end up going  when you fall and when it hurts and when  that path grows dark in that moment of  doubt and pain remember what I have come  to know deeply your future our future is  brighter than the darkness thank you
 

